# Data Privacy LGPD

Personal data handling must be intentional, minimal, traceable, and compatible with LGPD obligations.

## Classification

- Identify personal data, sensitive personal data, payment data, credentials, and business-confidential data before designing storage or logs.
- Keep the purpose and legal basis for personal data processing documented at the feature or domain level.
- Minimize collected fields to what the workflow actually needs.

## Storage And Access

- Encrypt sensitive data at rest when supported by the platform or database.
- Restrict access to personal data through explicit authorization policies.
- Do not copy PII into shared tables, analytics payloads, queues, or logs without a documented purpose.
- Tokenize or mask payment, document, phone, and email data when full value is not required.

## Retention And Deletion

- Define retention rules for personal data, audit logs, exports, and provider callbacks.
- Support anonymization or deletion workflows when legally required and technically safe.
- Avoid indefinite retention of raw third-party payloads.

## Observability And Exports

- Logs must not include passwords, tokens, full card data, full identity documents, or unnecessary PII.
- Data exports should be audited and limited to authorized roles.
- Test fixtures and seed data must not contain real customer data.
